"""Hand-written feature narratives for the style-only requirement variant."""

GITHUB = {
    'EV-1.1': '''Users can narrow the repository's issue list with a combined query instead of searching only for words in a title. On the Issues page, the “Issues” region presents the “Search issues” searchbox and the “Issue titles” list. The query accepts `is:open`, `is:closed`, `label:<name>` and `author:<username>` together; every condition must match. A status qualifier takes precedence over the currently selected status tab, while words without a qualifier continue to match the issue title. Matching titles are shown newest first, and excluded issues must not remain as result links. Refreshing this page retains the query and its matching results.''',
    'EV-1.2': '''Users can change the reading order of the issues they have already filtered. The “Issue sort” combobox on the Issues page offers “Newest” and “Oldest”; choosing an order changes the “Issue titles” list inside the “Issues” region without changing any issue. The order applies to the filtered result set, not to a separate unfiltered list. After refresh, the selector and the displayed titles still agree with the saved choice. Changing the selection again immediately shows the opposite chronological order.''',
    'EV-1.3': '''The Pull requests page supports finding drafts belonging to a particular author. Its “Pull requests” region provides the “Search pull requests” searchbox, and the visible result titles form the “Pull request titles” list. The search understands `is:draft`, `is:open` and `author:<username>`; conditions entered together are combined with AND. A draft is distinct from a ready Open pull request, even though both are unmerged. Filtering changes only the visible results, and refreshing the page preserves the query and the same matching pull-request links.''',
    'EV-1.4': '''The issue-closing workflow lets an authorized user explain that work will not be pursued. On the issue detail page, the “Issue” region has a “Close reason” combobox with “Completed” selected by default and “Not planned” as the other choice. Clicking the “Close issue” button still saves immediately, but now saves the chosen reason along with Closed status. A not-planned closure displays “Closed as not planned” as its status message. Clicking “Reopen issue” restores Open and removes that closure reason. These changes survive refresh, preserve the issue description and other existing data, and retain the original operation-specific permission rules.''',
    'EV-1.5': '''A user who may comment can inspect Markdown before publishing an issue comment. The comment editor in the “Issue” region has “Preview” and “Write” buttons beside the visibly labeled “Comment” field. Preview renders emphasis and other supported Markdown in the “Comment preview” region while keeping the draft unpublished. Write returns to the same editable draft. Only clicking the “Comment” submit button publishes the comment, once, into the “Discussion” region. The saved comment remains available after refresh; changing editor modes by itself must not add a discussion entry.''',
    'EV-1.6': '''A contributor creating a Markdown file can preview the document before committing it. The existing new-file editor retains its “File name”, “File contents” and “Commit message” fields and “Commit changes” submission. The “Preview file” button renders the draft in the “File preview” region, including Markdown headings, without creating a file or commit. “Edit file” returns to the retained contents. Committing then uses the original path validation, commit-message validation and write permissions, and the saved file can be reopened or refreshed with the same path and contents.''',
    'EV-1.7': '''An author can move an unmerged Open pull request back to Draft when it needs further work. The detail page's “Pull request” region offers “Convert to draft” to that author. Clicking it opens the “Convert to draft” confirmation dialog; its confirmation button has the same wording. Confirmation saves Draft without changing the title, description or source and target branches. The draft state survives refresh and keeps “Merge pull request” visibly disabled. The existing “Ready for review” workflow remains available to restore Open.''',
    'EV-1.8': '''Readers inspecting “Files changed” can choose how a pull-request diff is displayed. The “Diff layout” combobox offers “Unified” and “Split”, and its selection survives refresh without modifying the pull request or its files. Split places the old source text in the “Before” region and the new source text in the “After” region. Unified instead uses the “Unified diff” region, with removed and added lines carrying their minus and plus prefixes. Switching layouts keeps the same changed text and continues to support the original read-only diff workflow.''',
    'EV-1.9': '''An issue manager can find a label before applying it. Opening the “Labels” button in the “Issue” region reveals the repository's existing label options and a visibly labeled “Filter labels” textbox. Typing filters those options immediately and ignores letter case, so a nonmatching label is no longer offered while the filter is active. Selecting an exact matching label keeps the original immediate-save behavior: there is no additional Save action, and the selected label remains in the issue's metadata after refresh.''',
    'EV-1.10': '''The branch comparison page provides a “Swap branches” button alongside the existing “Base” and “Compare” comboboxes. Clicking it exchanges their current choices without writing either branch. The user can then click “Compare changes” to inspect that direction of comparison. The “Comparison summary” region identifies the selected base and compare branches using the wording shown in the scenario, and this comparison remains visible after refresh. The original comparison validation and pull-request creation workflow continue to apply.''',
    'EV-2.1': '''A signed-in user can star any readable repository from its overview. Clicking the “Star” button adds that account to the repository's stars and changes the available action to “Unstar”. The “Repository social” region displays the count of distinct starring accounts, using `1 star` for one account and `0 stars` when there are none. Refreshing preserves both the count and the current account's action. Unstar removes only that account's star, and the reduced count and restored Star action also persist.''',
    'EV-2.2': '''Repository stars belong to individual accounts. If one user stars a repository and signs out, the star remains in the repository's aggregate count. A different signed-in reader sees that count in the “Repository social” region but still has the “Star” button unless that reader has independently starred the repository. Returning to the first account restores its “Unstar” action and leaves the total unchanged. The account switches in this scenario use the ordinary sign-out confirmation and sign-in pages; they do not reset repository records.''',
    'EV-2.3': '''A repository administrator can maintain the topics displayed in the overview's “About” region. “Edit topics” opens the “Edit topics” dialog, where “Topics” is a visibly labeled text field accepting comma-separated entries and “Save” submits the change. Saving trims surrounding spaces, removes duplicates and converts topic names to lowercase; a topic name consists of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens. The resulting “Topics” list preserves the saved reading order. Leaving the repository and finding it again shows the same list, without creating separate topic copies for repeated input.''',
    'EV-2.4': '''A user with Read permission can inspect the repository topics but cannot change them. The overview's “About” region continues to show the saved “Topics” list in its existing order. The “Edit topics” button is absent for that reader, rather than offering an operation that fails only after submission. Refreshing does not reveal the editing control or hide the readable topic metadata.''',
    'EV-2.5': '''A repository administrator can archive an active repository from Settings and General. Clicking “Archive repository” opens the “Archive repository” dialog, which asks for the exact “Repository name” before the administrator clicks “Archive”. A successful confirmation persists Archived state and displays “Archived” in the “Repository status” region. Repository contents then become read-only, including issue creation, but existing issues remain readable. After refresh the archive marker is still present; opening Issues still shows the existing issue, while no “New issue” link or button is available.''',
    'EV-2.6': '''Archiving a public repository does not remove public read access. A visitor can open its overview, see the “Archived” marker in the “Repository status” region and follow an existing issue entry. The issue's title and saved description remain readable in the “Issue” region after refresh. Mutation controls, including “Close issue”, are absent for the visitor; archiving also leaves “New issue” unavailable. Reading the archived issue does not change its content or status.''',
    'EV-2.7': '''Administrators can add repository labels from the Labels page reached through Issues. “New label” opens the “New label” dialog with “Label name”, “Description” and “Color” fields and a “Create label” button. The name must be unique in the repository, and Color is a six-digit hexadecimal color value. Successful creation saves the label as a region identified by its name, where its description is readable. The same label is offered by the “Labels” picker on an issue in the “Issue” region. Applying it and refreshing retain the new label relationship.''',
    'EV-2.8': '''Administrators can rename a repository label that is already applied to an issue. On the Labels page, the label's own named region provides an “Edit” button, which opens the “Edit label” dialog. Editing “Label name” and “Description” and clicking “Save” updates the existing label record. Every issue association follows the rename atomically; it must not keep a second label with the old name. After refresh, the label's region uses its new name, and opening the linked issue shows that same name in the “Issue” region.''',
    'EV-2.9': '''Administrators can create a milestone on the Milestones page reached through Issues. The “New milestone” dialog is opened by “New milestone” and provides “Title”, “Description” and “Due date”; the date uses `YYYY-MM-DD`. “Create milestone” saves the record, and the milestone appears in a region identified by its title with the saved due date. It also becomes an option in an issue's “Milestone” picker. Selecting it saves the issue relationship immediately, and refreshing the issue keeps the milestone visible in the “Issue” region.''',
    'EV-2.10': '''An administrator can close a milestone and later reopen it from the Milestones page. Each milestone is displayed in a region identified by its title. For an open milestone, “Close milestone” saves Closed and replaces that operation with “Reopen milestone”; reopening saves Open. Both transitions survive refresh and leave the title, description, due date and linked issues unchanged. The milestone is not deleted or recreated by either operation.''',
    'EV-3.1': '''A repository administrator can stop ordinary commenting on an issue without removing the discussion. In the “Issue” region, “Lock conversation” opens the “Lock conversation” dialog with a “Lock reason” combobox and “Lock” confirmation. Saving records the selected reason and displays a persistent status such as “Locked: Off-topic”. Ordinary Write users can still read the issue and its existing discussion, but no “Comment” submission is available until an administrator unlocks it. Signing out and opening the issue as another writer must respect the saved lock.''',
    'EV-3.2': '''Conversation locks are managed only by repository administrators. A Read user opening the issue detail page can read the saved description in the “Issue” region, but neither “Lock conversation” nor “Unlock conversation” is available as a button. Refreshing the issue keeps the same read-only access. This restriction does not alter the issue's content or imply that a Read account may acquire lock-management permission from another role.''',
    'EV-3.3': '''An administrator can draw attention to an existing issue by clicking “Pin issue” in the “Issue” region. The repository's Issues page displays pinned issues above its ordinary results, in a separate “Pinned issues” region. Its “Pinned issue titles” list follows the order in which issues were pinned. Leaving the repository, returning through its normal entry and reopening Issues preserves the pin and the list entry. Pinning changes the repository's pin relationship, not the underlying issue or its discussion.''',
    'EV-3.4': '''An administrator can remove an existing issue pin by clicking “Unpin issue” in the “Issue” region. The detail page then offers “Pin issue” again, including after refresh. Returning to the Issues page shows that the title has left the “Pinned issue titles” list in the “Pinned issues” region; when no pins remain, that list is still visible but empty. The ordinary “Issues” region continues to provide the issue's link and discussion. Unpinning must not delete the issue.''',
    'EV-3.5': '''Any signed-in reader can add or remove a thumbs-up reaction to an issue. The “Thumbs up” button in the “Issue” region exposes whether the current account has pressed it. The separate “Reaction count” region shows the number of distinct reacting accounts, including `1 reaction` and `0 reactions`. A first click adds the account, and refresh preserves both its pressed state and the count. Clicking again removes that same account's reaction instead of adding another record.''',
    'EV-3.6': '''A signed-in user can maintain reusable replies through “Saved replies” in the account menu. “New saved reply” opens the “Saved reply” dialog, whose “Reply title” and “Reply body” fields are stored by “Save” for that account. On an issue, “Insert saved reply” offers the user's saved reply titles as selectable options. Choosing one fills the “Comment” field in the “Issue” region but does not publish anything. Clicking “Comment” then publishes the body through the existing comment workflow, and refresh preserves it in “Discussion”.''',
    'EV-3.7': '''A discussion comment's author can revise its body. The discussion article is identified as “Comment by <username>” and offers “Edit comment” to its author. This opens the “Edit comment” dialog, where the visibly labeled “Comment body” field initially contains the saved text. “Save comment” replaces that body while retaining the original author and adds an “Edited” indicator. After refresh the updated text and indicator remain in the same article, while the editing form has closed and “Save comment” is no longer present.''',
    'EV-3.8': '''A comment's author can remove it through a confirmation dialog. The “Comment by <username>” discussion article provides “Delete comment”, opening the “Delete comment” dialog. Clicking “Cancel” leaves the complete comment in the “Discussion” region. Repeating the operation and clicking “Delete” removes only that comment. After refresh its article remains absent, while the issue's own description is still readable in the “Issue” region.''',
    'EV-3.9': '''Markdown tasks in an issue description are interactive for users with Write permission. A task beginning with `- [ ]` is rendered as an unchecked checkbox labeled with its task text, while `- [x]` represents a completed task. The “Issue” region shows the number of completed tasks and the total. Toggling one checkbox updates both the saved Markdown and that progress, preserves every other task and surrounding text, and survives refresh. The existing “Edit issue description” operation still opens the “Issue description” field, allowing the saved newline-separated task text to be read.''',
    'EV-3.10': '''A review participant or the pull-request author can resolve an individual review conversation. Under “Files changed”, each thread is a region identified by its thread title. “Resolve conversation” marks that thread “Resolved” and changes the available operation to “Unresolve conversation”. The saved resolution survives refresh and leaves every comment readable. Unresolving also persists and restores “Resolve conversation”. These actions do not change the pull-request status or any approval decision.''',
}

SHEET = {
    'EV-1.1': '''The existing formula editor accepts conditional expressions using `IF(condition, true_value, false_value)`, comparison operators and quoted strings. A formula evaluates only its selected branch, while existing arithmetic and aggregate functions remain available. The user selects the destination cell, enters the expression in “Formula bar” and commits with Enter; the grid shows the result while the formula bar retains the expression. Editing the source cell recalculates the conditional result. Refreshing restores the saved source value, formula and current result.''',
    'EV-1.2': '''The formula engine supports `COUNTIF(range, criterion)` in addition to the existing aggregate functions. It counts cells matching the criterion, including numeric comparison prefixes and exact text comparisons that ignore letter case. The user enters the formula through “Formula bar” for a selected cell. Committing a change in the source range recalculates the count rather than retaining the original result, and refreshing preserves the source edit and the resulting count.''',
    'EV-1.3': '''Users can total matching records with `SUMIF(criteria_range, criterion, sum_range)`. The criterion accepts case-insensitive text matching or a numeric comparison; only numeric values in corresponding matching rows contribute to the sum. Entering the expression through “Formula bar” displays the calculated total in the destination cell. Editing a value on a nonmatching row must leave that total unchanged. Formula expressions, source values and results remain consistent after refresh.''',
    'EV-1.4': '''A formula can handle an error with `IFERROR(expression, fallback)`. The fallback is used only when the expression produces an error; once the source is corrected, the same formula returns its ordinary result without being rewritten. The user enters and edits values through “Formula bar”, and the worksheet displays the recalculated result. An unrelated error, such as `#DIV/0!` in another cell, remains visible. Refreshing preserves both the corrected result and any still-existing independent error.''',
    'EV-1.5': '''Formulas may refer to another worksheet using `SheetName!A1`, including a worksheet name enclosed in single quotes. The user can write such an expression in “Formula bar”, switch to the source worksheet's tab and edit the referenced value there. Returning to the dependent worksheet immediately shows its recalculated result. The source sheet's content must not replace the dependent sheet's content, and reopening or refreshing retains the formula and the result calculated from the latest source value.''',
    'EV-1.6': '''The existing “Sort range” operation supports a secondary key. Its dialog retains “Sort by”, “Order” and the “Data has header row” checkbox, and adds the optional “Then by” combobox, initially “None”, and “Then order”. Clicking “Sort” compares whole records by the primary column first, using the second column only for tied primary keys. The declared header stays in place, and cells outside the selected rectangle remain unchanged. The sorted records and their column alignment persist after refresh.''',
    'EV-1.7': '''Numeric and dropdown validation can warn instead of rejecting a user's value. The “Data validation” dialog retains its existing fields and adds the “On invalid data” combobox with “Reject input” selected by default and “Show warning” as the alternative. Reject input keeps the original complete-operation rejection behavior. With Show warning, an invalid value is committed and “Validation warning” is displayed when that cell is selected. Saving and reopening retain both the rule's parameters and the invalid-data policy; refreshing also preserves the committed value.''',
    'EV-1.8': '''The grid's context menu provides “Paste values only” alongside the existing copy and paste operations. After a user copies a rectangular selection, this command writes its calculated values into the selected destination with the same two-dimensional layout, removing formula expressions from the target cells. Source cells remain unchanged, and ordinary Paste continues to copy formulas. “Formula bar” therefore shows an ordinary value for a values-only target. Subsequent source edits recalculate the source formulas but leave those pasted values fixed, including after refresh.''',
    'EV-1.9': '''Workbook session undo history also covers worksheet renaming. The worksheet-options menu's “Rename” command opens the “Rename worksheet” dialog, with the current “Worksheet name” and a “Save” button. After a successful rename, “Undo” restores the previous tab name and keeps its data; “Redo” reapplies the saved name. These operations use the same workbook's session history and must not modify another workbook. The name and data visible after Redo remain after refresh.''',
    'EV-1.10': '''The formula language supports text concatenation with `&` and quoted strings. Cell text can be joined with literal text and numeric values; a numeric operand contributes the text of its displayed value. The selected cell's “Formula bar” retains the complete expression while the grid displays its combined result. Editing a referenced cell updates that result, and refresh preserves both the formula and the result produced from the current source values.''',
    'EV-2.1': '''Users can define a workbook-scoped name for a cell range through Data and “Named ranges”. This opens the “Named ranges” region; “Add named range” opens the “Named range” dialog with “Name”, an A1-style “Range” on the active worksheet, and “Save”. Range names are case-insensitive and are resolved by formulas throughout the workbook. Saving makes the definition available to an expression entered through “Formula bar”. Refreshing retains the definition, its original cells and the calculated formula result.''',
    'EV-2.2': '''An existing named range can be renamed without breaking its formulas. Data and “Named ranges” open the range-management region, where each saved range has its own region identified by its name and an “Edit” button. The “Named range” dialog permits changing “Name” and saving. Renaming updates every formula reference to the old name atomically; it does not change the selected range or the formulas' calculated values. Selecting a dependent cell shows the updated expression in “Formula bar”, including after refresh.''',
    'EV-2.3': '''Users can delete a named-range definition while preserving the underlying worksheet cells. The saved range's own named region in “Named ranges” provides “Delete”, followed by a “Delete named range” confirmation dialog and its “Delete” button. Once confirmed, formulas still referring to that now-unresolved name display `#NAME?`; their original expressions remain available in “Formula bar”. The source cells retain their values, and both the missing-name error and the unchanged source data persist after refresh.''',
    'EV-2.4': '''The “Find and replace” button opens the “Find and replace” dialog for the active worksheet. A user enters the literal search text in “Find” and may enable the “Match case” checkbox. “Find next” visits matching cells in row-major order, selects the matching cell and reports `Match <coordinate>` in the dialog. It wraps after the last match and never changes cell data. Clicking “Close” returns to the grid with that match selected, using the same selected-state semantics as ordinary cell selection.''',
    'EV-2.5': '''The active worksheet can be updated with a literal replacement through the “Find and replace” dialog. “Find” supplies the text to match, “Replace with” supplies its replacement, and “Replace all” applies every match while honoring “Match case”. The dialog reports the number of replacements. Successful replacement is atomic and remains after the user closes the dialog, navigates between worksheet tabs and refreshes. Cells on other worksheets keep their original values.''',
    'EV-2.6': '''Find and replace can operate on formula expressions as well as ordinary cell text. The “Search in formulas” checkbox in the “Find and replace” dialog enables matching against original expressions. With that option selected, “Replace all” updates matching expressions, reports the replacement count and recalculates their results. With it off, Replace all must not modify formula expressions. After closing the dialog or refreshing, the grid shows the new results and selecting a changed cell reveals the updated expression in “Formula bar”.''',
    'EV-2.7': '''“Duplicate” in a worksheet's options menu creates an independent copy immediately after the source and makes it active. The new tab is named “Copy of <name>”. Values, formulas and validation rules are copied with independent identities, so editing the duplicate does not change the source worksheet. Switching between the tabs restores each sheet's own values and results. The copy survives refresh, and reopening “Data validation” on a copied constrained cell shows the copied parameters.''',
    'EV-2.8': '''A worksheet can be hidden through “Hide” in its options menu without deleting its cells. Hiding the selected worksheet removes its visible tab and activates an adjacent visible worksheet, while ensuring that at least one worksheet stays visible. The hidden state and data persist after refresh. “Hidden sheets” opens the “Hidden sheets” dialog, where each retained hidden worksheet can be found through “Show <name>”. The newly active sheet continues to display its own data.''',
    'EV-2.9': '''Users can restore a hidden worksheet by opening “Hidden sheets” and clicking its “Show <name>” button in the “Hidden sheets” dialog. The worksheet returns to its original position among the tabs and becomes active. Its stored cells are restored without copying data from the previously active worksheet. Refreshing keeps the restored tab, its selected state and its saved values.''',
    'EV-2.10': '''The worksheet-options menu provides “Move left” to move a worksheet one position toward the start of the tab bar. The operation changes only its order; its active state and stored cells remain unchanged. The visible order is also represented by the “Worksheet order” list, whose entries are the worksheet names in tab order. Refreshing or reopening the workbook preserves that order and the same active worksheet.''',
    'EV-3.1': '''A user can attach a note to the selected cell without changing its value. “Insert note” opens the “Cell note” dialog, with a visibly labeled “Note” field and “Save”. Once saved, the cell provides “Show note for <coordinate>” to reopen the stored note. The note remains after refresh, and its dialog can be closed through “Close” to inspect the unchanged cell value.''',
    'EV-3.2': '''A cell's existing note can be edited or removed independently of the cell's value. “Show note for <coordinate>” opens the “Cell note” dialog with the stored “Note” text. “Save” replaces the note and persists the revised text after refresh. “Delete note” removes it and closes the dialog; after refresh the corresponding Show note control is absent, while the original grid value remains unchanged.''',
    'EV-3.3': '''Data and “Trim whitespace” clean only ordinary text cells inside the user's selected rectangle. The operation removes leading and trailing ASCII spaces and collapses each internal run of ASCII spaces to one; formulas and cells outside the rectangle are left unchanged. It is a single undoable operation. “Formula bar” allows the user to inspect the complete saved text, including any spaces intentionally retained outside the selection. Undo restores the original text, Redo reapplies the cleanup, and refreshing preserves the successful cleanup.''',
    'EV-3.4': '''The user can split the contents of a selected column through Data and “Split text to columns”. Its dialog has a “Separator” combobox and a “Split” button. Choosing “Comma” separates each selected cell into adjacent columns on the same row, preserving empty fields and the order of the original rows. The change is committed as one undoable operation, remains after refresh and does not affect cells outside the destination rectangle described in the scenario.''',
    'EV-3.5': '''Data and “Remove duplicates” remove repeated records within a selected rectangle. The “Remove duplicates” dialog has a “Data has header row” checkbox and a confirmation button also called “Remove duplicates”. Records are compared using exact values across all selected columns; the first copy of each record remains in its original relative order. Any declared header stays in place, trailing cells within the selected rectangle are cleared and outside cells are untouched. The application reports the number removed, and refresh preserves the remaining records.''',
    'EV-3.6': '''Users can keep the top rows in view while scrolling the active worksheet. View and “Freeze rows” open the “Freeze rows” dialog, where “Rows to freeze” accepts a nonnegative integer and “Save” applies it. That many top rows stay fixed during vertical grid scrolling. The “Frozen rows” region reports the saved count. The setting belongs to the current worksheet: refreshing retains it, and switching to another tab shows that sheet's own independently saved setting.''',
    'EV-3.7': '''A column-header context menu offers “Hide column”. Hiding removes that column header and its grid cells from the visible worksheet but retains their values and their role in formula references. Dependent formulas continue to calculate from those values. A “Show column <letter>” button is available for each hidden column. Refreshing preserves the hidden state without deleting or shifting the underlying data.''',
    'EV-3.8': '''“Show column <letter>” restores a hidden column at its original position in the worksheet. Its column header and grid cells become visible again, with their saved values unchanged. Formulas that refer to those cells continue to show the correct result before and after restoration. Refreshing preserves the restored column and its contents.''',
    'EV-3.9': '''The View menu has a “Show formulas” menuitemcheckbox. Checking it displays the original expression in formula cells instead of the calculated result; ordinary values continue to display normally. Unchecking it restores calculated results. This setting changes only presentation and belongs to the current worksheet. After refresh the saved setting is still checked or unchecked as appropriate, and the View menu exposes that same checked state.''',
    'EV-3.10': '''“Make a copy” opens the “Copy workbook” dialog with “Workbook name” and “Create copy”. Creating the copy duplicates all worksheets, formulas, notes and rules into a separate workbook and opens it under its new heading. Editing its values recalculates its own formulas without changing the source workbook. Both records remain available through their workbook-name links on the home page; reopening the original and the copy, or refreshing the copy, shows their separate saved states.''',
}
